{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794588796,"nonce":"CMCAYwo8YeBTVISkiYB8MPa/3fs=","title":"Cooperative future tests assume work fits one slice under Miri","message":"On macOS arm64 with rustc/Miri e7769602a (2026-08-24), cargo miri test -p hxn-future reports 30 passes and four failures. Each failing assertion expects Resolved but sees Pending:\n\n- tests::a_step_may_queue_work_and_may_not_drive_it\n- tests::a_wait_without_limit_finishes_short_work_and_gives_up_on_long\n- tests::queued_async_and_stepped_work_share_the_queue\n- tests::queued_work_takes_turns\n\nAll four reproduce individually against a clean git archive of 16da3fa, before the uncommitted record-default changes. Reproducer: cargo miri test -p hxn-future queued_work_takes_turns. Each isolated baseline invocation was run under a 120-second timeout and failed with the same Pending/Resolved assertion in under one second. The full suite finished in 470 seconds. Ordinary workspace tests pass on macOS and the Linux NUC; runtime fixture repetitions also pass on both machines.\n\nlocal::drive(None, ...) returns after its 4 ms SLICE rather than promising that finite work has finished. These tests assume that a single wait_driving(None) completes their work. Miri's execution/clock overhead appears to consume the slice before that happens; this timing explanation is an inference from the code and the failures. A correction should keep the scheduler's host-return budget intact and verify fairness/completion without assuming that the whole sequence fits in one slice. The endless-work budget test must still check that control returns to the host.\n\nNo hxn-future source changed while finding this. hxn-abi's Miri suite passes all 35 tests. Its only pending source change is a wire-format documentation line for defaulted record fields. This is separate from the earlier lost-wake defect and from the closed ABI Miri coverage issue.","files":null}]}